      "math_academy_id": "q-331879",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}={{field-2}} \\\\ {{field-3}}=2{X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=4L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+3L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-1=4{X}_{1}(s)-{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+2=2{X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively.",
